Expo SDK 58 made **data loaders** stable in Expo Router. A route file can now export a `loader` function that runs **on the server**, and the screen reads its result with the `useLoaderData` hook. No experimental flag, no separate API layer for simple reads.

It's the same idea as loaders in Remix and React Router, but in a framework that also targets iOS and Android, so there are a few things that work differently from the web. This tutorial covers the setup, typed loaders, errors, caching, and what happens in native apps, with every snippet checked against `expo-router` 58 and `expo-server` 58. (If you haven't upgraded yet, start with my [Expo SDK 58 upgrade guide](/blog/expo-sdk-58-upgrade-guide).)

![How a data loader works: on the web, the server runs the loader and embeds the data in the HTML; on later navigation and in native apps, the client fetches /_expo/loaders/<route> and the server runs the loader again; secrets stay on the server in both cases](/blog/expo-router-data-loaders-tutorial/loader-flow.jpg)

## Why use data loaders?

- **Secrets stay on the server.** A loader can use `process.env` API keys, and `loader` exports are removed from the client bundle. Anything shipped inside an app can be extracted, so this matters (see [how to keep AI API keys out of your React Native app](/blog/react-native-ai-api-key-security)).
- **Data arrives with the page on the web.** With server or static rendering, the loader's result is embedded in the HTML, so the first render already has data. That's good for speed and for search engines.
- **Less code.** No `useEffect`, loading flags, and an API route just to read data for one screen. The loader lives next to the screen that uses it, and `useLoaderData<typeof loader>()` infers its type.

## Setup

Loaders need static or server output for the web. SDK 58 projects already default to `"server"` when `web.output` isn't set, but setting it explicitly makes the choice visible:

```json
{
  "expo": {
    "web": {
      "output": "server"
    }
  }
}
```

Pick the mode based on when your data changes:

| | Static (`"static"`) | Server (`"server"`) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Loader runs | Once, at `npx expo export` | On every request |
| `request` argument | `undefined` | The incoming request |
| Good for | Docs, marketing, content that changes with deploys | Personalized or frequently changing data |

In SDK 55 to 57, loaders were experimental and needed `unstable_useServerDataLoaders` in the `expo-router` plugin. In SDK 58 that flag is no longer needed.

## Your first loader

Here's a product list that calls a private API with a server-side key:

```tsx
// src/app/products/index.tsx
import { Suspense } from "react";
import { Text, View } from "react-native";
import { Link, useLoaderData } from "expo-router";
import type { LoaderFunction } from "expo-router/server";

type Product = { id: string; name: string; price: number };

export const loader: LoaderFunction<Product[]> = async () => {
  const response = await fetch("https://api.example.com/products", {
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.STORE_API_KEY}` },
  });
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Products request failed: ${response.status}`);
  }
  return response.json();
};

export default function ProductsScreen() {
  return (
    <View>
      <Text>Products</Text>
      <Suspense fallback={<Text>Loading products…</Text>}>
        <ProductList />
      </Suspense>
    </View>
  );
}

function ProductList() {
  const products = useLoaderData<typeof loader>(); // Product[]
  return products.map((product) => (
    <Link key={product.id} href={`/products/${product.id}`}>
      {product.name} · ${product.price}
    </Link>
  ));
}
```

A few things to notice:

- **`STORE_API_KEY` has no `EXPO_PUBLIC_` prefix.** Only `EXPO_PUBLIC_` variables are inlined into the app bundle; this one is read on the server, inside the loader.
- **`useLoaderData` suspends while data is loading.** Wrap the component that calls it in `<Suspense>` and you control exactly where the loading state appears. The hook works in any component inside the route, not only the route component.
- **Typing the loader with `LoaderFunction<Product[]>`** gives `useLoaderData<typeof loader>()` the right type, even though `response.json()` returns `any`.

## Dynamic routes and typed params

Loaders receive the route params. The `createStaticLoader` helper from `expo-router/server` types a loader that only needs params, and it works with both static and server output:

```tsx
// src/app/products/[id].tsx
import { Text, View } from "react-native";
import { useLoaderData, type ErrorBoundaryProps } from "expo-router";
import { createStaticLoader } from "expo-router/server";
import { StatusError } from "expo-server";

type Product = { id: string; name: string; price: number };

export const loader = createStaticLoader(async (params) => {
  const id = params.id;
  if (typeof id !== "string") {
    throw new StatusError(400, "Invalid product id");
  }

  const response = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/products/${encodeURIComponent(id)}`, {
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.STORE_API_KEY}` },
  });
  if (response.status === 404) {
    throw new StatusError(404, "Product not found");
  }
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Product request failed: ${response.status}`);
  }
  return (await response.json()) as Product;
});

export function ErrorBoundary({ retry }: ErrorBoundaryProps) {
  return (
    <View>
      <Text>We couldn't load this product.</Text>
      <Text onPress={retry}>Try again</Text>
    </View>
  );
}

export default function ProductScreen() {
  const product = useLoaderData<typeof loader>();
  return (
    <View>
      <Text>{product.name}</Text>
      <Text>${product.price}</Text>
    </View>
  );
}
```

Two details that trip people up:

- **Params are typed `string | string[]`**, because catch-all routes like `[...slug]` produce arrays. Narrow the value before you use it, as above.
- **A thrown error goes to the route's `ErrorBoundary`.** When the client fetches loader data and the server responds with an error, the client sees a generic `Failed to fetch loader data: 404` error, not your `StatusError` message. So show a user-friendly message in the boundary instead of rendering `error.message`. `StatusError` from `expo-server` still matters: it sets the status code of the loader's HTTP response, so caches and your server logs see a real 404 instead of a generic failure.

## Caching loader responses

Since SDK 58, Expo Router doesn't keep loader data in a permanent in-memory cache. When you return to a route after it unmounts, `useLoaderData` fetches again, and the platform's HTTP cache decides whether to reuse a response, based on `Cache-Control`. Without a header, loader responses default to `no-store` on the server.

To cache, return a `Response` with a JSON body and a `Cache-Control` header:

```tsx
import { Text } from "react-native";
import { useLoaderData } from "expo-router";
import type { LoaderFunction } from "expo-router/server";

type Product = { id: string; name: string; price: number };

export async function loader() {
  const response = await fetch("https://api.example.com/products");
  const products: Product[] = await response.json();

  return Response.json(products, {
    headers: { "Cache-Control": "public, max-age=60" },
  });
}

function ProductList() {
  // typeof loader would infer Response here, so name the data type instead.
  const products = useLoaderData<LoaderFunction<Product[]>>();
  return <Text>{products.length} products</Text>;
}
```

The comment is the catch: **when a loader returns a `Response`, `useLoaderData<typeof loader>()` infers `Response`**, even though the hook gives you the parsed JSON at runtime. Pass `LoaderFunction<YourData>` as the type parameter instead.

And the rule that matters most: **use `public` only for data that's the same for every user.** For anything personal, use `private` or `no-store`, or a shared cache can serve one user's data to another.

## Personalized data with createServerLoader

With server output, a loader can read the incoming request: headers, cookies, the URL. `createServerLoader` types the request as always present, and throws if you accidentally use it during a static export:

```tsx
// src/app/account/orders.tsx
import { createServerLoader } from "expo-router/server";
import { setResponseHeaders, StatusError } from "expo-server";

type Order = { id: string; total: number };

export const loader = createServerLoader(async (request) => {
  const cookie = request.headers.get("Cookie");
  if (!cookie) {
    throw new StatusError(401, "Sign in required");
  }

  setResponseHeaders({ "Cache-Control": "private, no-store" });

  const response = await fetch("https://api.example.com/me/orders", {
    headers: { Cookie: cookie },
  });
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new StatusError(response.status, "Could not load orders");
  }
  return (await response.json()) as Order[];
});
```

This works naturally on the web, where the browser sends its cookies with the page request and with same-origin fetches. On native, read the next section first.

## Data loaders in native apps

On iOS and Android, a loader **still runs on the server**, never on the phone. There's no server rendering on native, so the first time a route renders, `useLoaderData` fetches `/_expo/loaders/<route>` from your server and suspends until the data arrives. That's why the `<Suspense>` boundary matters even more on native.

What this means in practice:

- **In development**, the app fetches from the dev server that `npx expo start` runs, so it just works.
- **In production, you need a deployed server.** Deploy it (for example with EAS Hosting), and point the app at it with the `origin` option of the `expo-router` config plugin. Expo also has an experimental way to deploy the server automatically during `eas build`; its docs mark native deployment as alpha.
- **Version your server with your app.** Old app builds stay on people's phones for months. If a store build expects a loader's data in one shape and you deploy a server that returns another, old builds break. Keep loader responses backward compatible, the same way you would with any API.
- **Authentication is your job.** The router fetches loader data with a plain request that only adds an `Accept: application/json` header; it doesn't attach your app's auth token. For signed-in data in a native app, I'd use an API route or your own backend with an explicit token until you've tested exactly how your auth reaches the loader.

## Known limitations

From the Expo docs:

- Loaders must return JSON-serializable data or a `Response` with a JSON body. **Streaming loader data isn't supported**, so for AI responses use an API route ([how to stream AI responses in React Native](/blog/react-native-stream-ai-responses)).
- There's **no public API to invalidate** loader data for a route that stays mounted.
- A `loader` in a native-only file such as `index.ios.tsx` doesn't run. Put it in `index.web.tsx` or `index.tsx`.

## When I'd use loaders, and when I wouldn't

**I'd use data loaders for:**

- Expo apps that also ship a **web version**, where server rendering and SEO matter.
- **Public, read-heavy screens:** catalogs, articles, help content, anything cacheable.
- Screens that need a **secret** to read data and don't justify a separate backend endpoint.

**I'd keep a client data library (like TanStack Query) and my own API for:**

- **Mobile-first apps with offline needs,** where the app should show cached data without a network.
- **Frequent mutations and optimistic updates,** which loaders don't handle.
- **Signed-in data on native,** for the authentication reasons above.

Many apps will use both: loaders for public, web-facing routes, and a client data layer for the signed-in experience.

## FAQ

### Are Expo Router data loaders stable?

Yes. Data loaders, server rendering and middleware are stable as of Expo SDK 58 and no longer need experimental flags. In SDK 55 to 57, you had to enable `unstable_useServerDataLoaders`.

### Do data loaders run on the device?

No. Loaders always run on the server. On the web, the data is embedded in the server-rendered HTML; on native, the app fetches it from your deployed server.

### Can I use secrets and API keys in a loader?

Yes. Loaders run on the server and `loader` exports are removed from the client bundle, so `process.env` secrets aren't exposed. Don't give secrets the `EXPO_PUBLIC_` prefix, which inlines them into the app.

### Why does useLoaderData return a Response type?

Because your loader returns `Response.json(...)`, and `typeof loader` infers its return type. Use `useLoaderData<LoaderFunction<YourData>>()` to type the parsed data instead.
